utcomes/reading-the-mastery-report","title":"Reading the mastery report","description":"Read outcome evidence, filters, confidence, and gaps without confusing a report signal with a final instructional decision. Learn the key checks and safe next.","bodyMd":"::: key-takeaways\n- Read outcome evidence, filters, confidence, and gaps without confusing a report signal with a final instructional decision.\n- Confirm the available controls in your own organization because permissions and enabled features determine what appears.\n- Use a test record or limited pilot first, then review the resulting learner and administrator experience.\n:::\n\n::: answer\nRead outcome evidence, filters, confidence, and gaps without confusing a report signal with a final instructional decision. Open the relevant settings, confirm your role permits the change, test with limited scope, and review the result from the affected user's view. Keep organization policy and learner privacy in the approval path.\n:::\n\n## Who can use this feature?\n\nThis guide applies to **instructor, admin** roles in K–12, higher education, and homeschool settings. The exact control is shown only when the signed-in person has the required permission and the capability is enabled for the organization. Global access does not replace course or organization policy. If the named page or action is absent, ask an administrator to confirm permissions before changing configuration.\n\n## How do you complete the task?\n\n::: steps\n1. Open the course, account, or organization area named by the feature and confirm that you are working in the intended scope.\n2. Review the current value and any dependent settings before editing it. Record the starting state when a rollback may be needed.\n3. Make the smallest required change, using synthetic or limited test data when the workflow affects people, grades, identity, or integrations.\n4. Save the change and verify it from the affected role's view. Check both the expected result and access that should remain unavailable.\n5. Communicate the change when it alters learner access, deadlines, grading, sign-in, data handling, or a connected system.\n:::\n\nLextures exposes only controls supported by the deployed version. Self-hosted operators should compare their release and configuration with the current project source.[^1] Screens and labels can change between releases, so the verification step is part of the procedure rather than an optional cleanup task.\n\n## What should you check afterward?\n\nConfirm that the expected person can see or perform the intended action and that unrelated users cannot. Review any audit, delivery, synchronization, or status information the feature provides. For a workflow that exchanges data, compare a small sample at both ends instead of assuming that a successful request means every record was applied.\n\nKeep a support-safe note of the scope, time, and outcome. Do not copy passwords, tokens, accommodation details, or unnecessary learner records into that note. For broader context, visit the [help center](/docs), the [outcomes help category](/docs/outcomes), and [Lextures platform overview](/platform). See also [Aligning assignments to standards and outcomes](/docs/outcomes/aligning-assignments-to-standards), [Creating course outcomes](/docs/outcomes/creating-course-outcomes), and [Exporting outcome and mastery results](/docs/outcomes/exporting-outcome-results).\n\nThe public platform overview provides the product context used by this help article.[^2]\n\n::: faq\n### Why can I not see this setting?\nYour role, organization policy, course state, or deployed version may not expose it. Confirm that you are in the correct organization or course, then ask an administrator to review the relevant permission and feature configuration. Avoid working around a missing control with shared credentials.\n\n### Should I test the change first?\nYes. Use the smallest realistic scope and synthetic data where possible. For identity, roster, grading, accommodation, payment, or integration changes, verify both the intended path and a user who should remain unaffected before expanding the configuration.\n\n### What information should I send to support?\nSend the page name, approximate time, your role, the affected scope, and a description of expected and observed behavior. Remove learner records and secrets. Include a screenshot only when it contains synthetic data or has been reviewed and redacted.\n:::\n\n[^1]: https://github.com/lextures/lextures\n[^2]: https://lextures.com/platform","author":"chase-willden","reviewer":null,"publishedAt":"2026-08-11","contentUpdatedAt":"2026-08-11","reviewedAt":null,"reviewDueOn":"2027-02-11","primaryQuestion":"How do I use reading the mastery report in Lextures?","cluster":"help-outcomes","pillar":"","briefRef":"","verifiedAgainst":"2026-08-11 source","keywords":["Lextures","outcomes","help"],"relatedTo":["/docs/outcomes/aligning-assignments-to-standards","/docs/outcomes/creating-course-outcomes","/docs/outcomes/exporting-outcome-results"],"roles":["instructor","admin"],"segments":["k12","higher-ed","homeschool"],"citations":[],"extra":{"import":{"sourcePath":"www/src/docs/outcomes/reading-the-mastery-report.md","gitSha":"079aacf8fef3b171a54b2063dce723471714b824","importedAt":"2026-08-12T00:00:00Z","lastmodSource":"frontmatter","sourceHash":"b08a36c7018a8f57350183d8bad20744f54fae64efea7c9849b195ecaaa226e4","importedRevision":1,"metadata":{"contentContract":"answer-first-v1","supportTicketThemes":["outcomes-reading-the-mastery-report"]}},"seedMigration":"485"}},{"kind":"doc","slug":"following-multiple-children","category":"parents","path":"/docs/parents/following-multiple-children","title":"Following multiple children","description":"Add authorized learner relationships and switch between children while keeping each course and progress view distinct. Learn the key checks and safe next step.","bodyMd":"::: key-takeaways\n- Add authorized learner relationships and switch between children while keeping each course and progress view distinct.\n- Confirm the available controls in your own organization because permissions and enabled features determine what appears.\n- Use a test record or limited pilot first, then review the resulting learner and administrator experience.\n:::\n\n::: answer\nAdd authorized learner relationships and switch between children while keeping each course and progress view distinct. Open the relevant settings, confirm your role permits the change, test with limited scope, and review the result from the affected user's view. Keep organization policy and learner privacy in the approval path.\n:::\n\n## Who can use this feature?\n\nThis guide applies to **parent, admin** roles in K–12, higher education, and homeschool settings. The exact control is shown only when the signed-in person has the required permission and the capability is enabled for the organization. Global access does not replace course or organization policy. If the named page or action is absent, ask an administrator to confirm permissions before changing configuration.\n\n## How do you complete the task?\n\n::: steps\n1. Open the course, account, or organization area named by the feature and confirm that you are working in the intended scope.\n2. Review the current value and any dependent settings before editing it. Record the starting state when a rollback may be needed.\n3. Make the smallest required change, using synthetic or limited test data when the workflow affects people, grades, identity, or integrations.\n4. Save the change and verify it from the affected role's view. Check both the expected result and access that should remain unavailable.\n5. Communicate the change when it alters learner access, deadlines, grading, sign-in, data handling, or a connected system.\n:::\n\nLextures exposes only controls supported by the deployed version. Self-hosted operators should compare their release and configuration with the current project source.[^1] Screens and labels can change between releases, so the verification step is part of the procedure rather than an optional cleanup task.\n\n## What should you check afterward?\n\nConfirm that the expected person can see or perform the intended action and that unrelated users cannot. Review any audit, delivery, synchronization, or status information the feature provides. For a workflow that exchanges data, compare a small sample at both ends instead of assuming that a successful request means every record was applied.\n\nKeep a support-safe note of the scope, time, and outcome. Do not copy passwords, tokens, accommodation details, or unnecessary learner records into that note. For broader context, visit the [help center](/docs), the [parents help category](/docs/parents), and [Lextures platform overview](/platform). See also [Setting up the parent portal](/docs/parents/setting-up-parent-portal), [Pairing a parent to a student account](/docs/parents/pairing-parent-and-student), and [Managing parent notifications](/docs/parents/managing-parent-notifications).\n\nThe public platform overview provides the product context used by this help article.[^2]\n\n::: faq\n### Why can I not see this setting?\nYour role, organization policy, course state, or deployed version may not expose it. Confirm that you are in the correct organization or course, then ask an administrator to review the relevant permission and feature configuration. Avoid working around a missing control with shared credentials.\n\n### Should I test the change first?\nYes. Use the smallest realistic scope and synthetic data where possible. For identity, roster, grading, accommodation, payment, or integration changes, verify both the intended path and a user who should remain unaffected before expanding the configuration.\n\n### What information should I send to support?\nSend the page name, approximate time, your role, the affected scope, and a description of expected and observed behavior. Remove learner records and secrets. Include a screenshot only when it contains synthetic data or has been reviewed and redacted.\n:::\n\n[^1]: https://github.com/lextures/lextures\n[^2]: https://lextures.com/platform","author":"chase-willden","reviewer":null,"publishedAt":"2026-08-11","contentUpdatedAt":"2026-08-11","reviewedAt":null,"reviewDueOn":"2027-02-11","primaryQuestion":"How do I use following multiple children in Lextures?","cluster":"help-parents","pillar":"","briefRef":"","verifiedAgainst":"2026-08-11 source","keywords":["Lextures","parents","help"],"relatedTo":["/docs/parents/setting-up-parent-portal","/docs/parents/pairing-parent-and-student","/docs/parents/managing-parent-notifications"],"roles":["parent","admin"],"segments":["k12","higher-ed","homeschool"],"citations":[],"extra":{"import":{"sourcePath":"www/src/docs/parents/following-multiple-children.md","gitSha":"079aacf8fef3b171a54b2063dce723471714b824","importedAt":"2026-08-12T00:00:00Z","lastmodSource":"frontmatter","sourceHash":"a339e03bebe99837b740377255e43535dc4c4fdfd1d94f96ce413e015212228b","importedRevision":1,"metadata":{"contentContract":"answer-first-v1","supportTicketThemes":["parents-following-multiple-children"]}},"seedMigration":"485"}},{"kind":"doc","slug":"managing-parent-notifications","category":"parents","path":"/docs/parents/managing-parent-notifications","title":"Managing parent notifications","description":"Choose guardian notification topics and cadence while respecting organization policy and learner privacy. Learn the key checks and safe next steps..","bodyMd":"::: key-takeaways\n- Choose guardian notification topics and cadence while respecting organization policy and learner privacy.\n- Confirm the available controls in your own organization because permissions and enabled features determine what appears.\n- Use a test record or limited pilot first, then review the resulting learner and administrator experience.\n:::\n\n::: answer\nChoose guardian notification topics and cade